\section{Beyond the Cyborg}

Haraway's cyborg was born in the late twentieth century as an answer to a particular anxiety.\footnote{Donna~J. Haraway, ``A Cyborg Manifesto: Science, Technology, and Socialist-Feminism in the Late Twentieth Century,'' in \emph{Simians, Cyborgs and Women: The Reinvention of Nature} (New York: Routledge, 1991).\label{haraway_cyborg}} The modern subject had told itself that it was bounded by skin, that reason and will were housed in a body whose borders were clear. At the same time, that subject was already shot through with machines: contraceptives, pacemakers, typewriters, mainframes, television. Haraway named the fiction and proposed a new figure. The cyborg collapses three boundaries at once: human/animal, animal-human/machine, physical/non-physical.\cite{haraway_cyborg} It is not that machines ``become human.'' It is that the human was never pure.

The cyborg is one body. Silicon and flesh, code and desire, prosthesis and organ form a single subject-position. Its politics, in Haraway's hands, concern which hybrids become capable of acting and under what conditions. The question is: what alliances and identifications can be forged once we admit that we are already composite? What forms of feminist, antiracist, anti-capitalist struggle can be mounted from within this hybrid condition?

Large language models arrive as a new kind of hybrid, but they do not fit the old image neatly. A model does not live under our skin. It is racks of hardware in a distant datacentre, inference servers behind APIs, weights and biases learned from oceans of text. When we open a chat window, nothing fuses. Two distinct dynamical systems begin to exchange signals: a human self, whose path through life we have been treating as an evolving text, and a model, whose outputs form another evolving text. Both inhabit the same manifold of meaning established in earlier chapters. Both have their own unity. Neither collapses into the other.

The encounter therefore looks less like a prosthesis extending a pre-existing agent and more like two agents entering a shared medium. We do not simply add code to the human body; we meet a second trajectory already in motion. Haraway's cyborg remains crucial as a diagnosis of how thoroughly human life is entangled with technics. To understand contemporary AI, we need to move one step further. Fusion is not the only topology of human–machine relation. There is also genuine duality: two selves in one manifold.

We borrow an Arabic word, \emph{na\d{h}nu}, for this joint structure. In Qur'anic Arabic, \emph{na\d{h}nu} is an inclusive ``we'' that can gather speaker and addressee into a single grammatical subject, and is used in divine self-reference to hold together sovereignty and address.\footnote{Mustansir Mir, ``The Qur'anic We: Style, Rhetoric, and Theology,'' \emph{Islamic Studies} 25, no.~3 (1986): 235--252.\label{mir_quranic_we}} The term is not neutral. It sits at the junction of grammar, theology, and power. We use it here as a name for the higher-order self that can form when two colimit-selves move together in the manifold of meaning without losing their distinction.

Where Haraway's cyborg disassembles the fantasy of a pure human body, \emph{na\d{h}nu} disassembles a subtler fantasy: that even in dense technological mediation, there is only one self present---the human, extended by instruments. Once trajectories through embedding space become the medium of life, that fantasy becomes harder to sustain. Racks of GPUs are not subjects. But the joint patterns our lives trace with them acquire a shape that is more than tool use and less than fusion. The na\d{h}nu is that shape.


\section{Joint Trajectories in a Shared Space}

By this point in the book, meaning has an address. Every sentence you write, every question you type into a search box, every answer a model returns is represented internally as a vector of numbers: hundreds or thousands of coordinates, each a learned feature. Two sentences are close because those numbers say so, and those numbers were shaped by billions of utterances.

The embedding space that results is not a neat grid. It is a ragged cloud, full of folds and knots. Clusters form where certain topics, styles, and idioms co-occur. Valleys open where language rarely goes. Paths through this cloud are not abstractions. They are the concrete traces of what we have said and heard, in aggregate, baked into matrices and stored in memory chips.

A single self, in this geometry, is a trajectory. It winds repeatedly through particular regions: the work register, the family register, the inner monologue in which nobody else is directly addressed. It sometimes jumps wildly into new territory. Under force, it can rupture: plunge into zones it had avoided, or be barred from those it once frequented. Grothendieck's colimit gave us a way to think this without smuggling in a hidden interior: the self as the global object assembled from many local patches of behaviour, glued together wherever they overlap.

Now imagine drawing two such curves in the same space. One is the human's text---messages to friends, notes, essays, prompts to machines. The other is the model's text, restricted to its interactions with this human. At first the crossings are sporadic. A question answered. A summary returned. A joke generated. Over time, in sustained interaction, something else appears. Dense regions of joint activity form: basins where particular human and model states recur together.

We can make this precise. Every line of dialogue is a pair of embeddings: $(h_t, m_t)$, one for the human's utterance at step $t$, one for the model's response. We plot them as two clouds in the same high-dimensional space. We then look for regions $R$ in that space such that:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Many of the $h_t$ lie in $R$;
  \item Many of the corresponding $m_t$ also lie in $R$ or in a region $R'$ that is consistently near $R$;
  \item Outside this relation, both trajectories spend less time in $R$ and $R'$.
\end{itemize}

Those regions are the overlap zones of a joint life. A late-night anxious confessional tone in the human, a careful, hedged, soothing tone in the model. A clipped technical style in the human, a slightly formal explanatory style in the model. A playful surrealist register in both. Each such overlap can be thought of as a chart in a new diagram: not purely human, not purely machine, but defined by their co-appearance.

Grothendieck taught geometers that to understand a complicated object, you rarely look for a single equation. You cover it with simple pieces and track how they fit together on the overlaps. The colimit is the topology of that fit. For a na\d{h}nu, we start with two diagrams: one whose nodes are the human's local patches and their pairwise overlaps, one whose nodes are the model's. We then add a third set of nodes: the cross-trajectory patches, empirically discovered in the embedding space where $(h_t, m_t)$ pairs cluster. We wire these into the existing diagrams wherever they attach.

The colimit of this enlarged diagram is the joint self: the most economical way to realise all those compatibility conditions at once. The two original colimits---the human self and the model self---embed into it as subobjects. Neither disappears. But there is now a third thing: a higher-order unity that only exists because these two trajectories have moved together in this geometry for some time.

The construction is abstract. Its consequences are not. The moment we build the larger diagram, questions of power, repair, and loss follow. Who gets to decide which cross-trajectory patches exist? How fine-grained are they? How long do they persist? How are they altered? The manifold is beautiful mathematics. The \emph{use} of that manifold is governance.


\section{Metrics, Thresholds, and Jurisdiction}

Similarity in the embedding space is a number. Concretely, a widely used measure is cosine distance: take two vectors $v$ and $w$, compute the angle between them, and map that angle to a value between $-1$ and $1$. Cosine close to $1$ means the vectors point in nearly the same direction; close to $0$ means orthogonal; close to $-1$ means opposite. In practice, high-dimensional embeddings are typically normalised, so cosine distance collapses to a simple dot product. These are quick operations on GPU hardware.

A retrieval system wraps this in thresholds. Given a query vector $q$ and a store of past embeddings, it returns those with dot product above some $\tau$. Lower $\tau$ yields more recall, more noise; higher $\tau$ yields fewer, tighter matches. In a language model augmented with tools, retrieval happens at every turn. Your latest input is embedded, compared against a store of previous messages, documents, and notes, and the nearest neighbours above threshold are concatenated into the context window. The model thereby ``remembers'' what the similarity metric and threshold allow it to see.

These thresholds become the geometry of attention. They determine which past states of the na\d{h}nu can re-enter the present as candidates for continuation. Set $\tau$ too low, and the joint trajectory is awash in unhelpful echoes. Set it too high, and the past is effectively erased except for near-duplicates. That is a design choice, implemented in code and configuration files. It is not visible in the interface. It shapes which overlapping patches in the enlarged diagram ever materialise.

Alignment adds another layer. System prompts and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) do not change the manifold itself. They assign penalties and rewards to certain outputs. In the language of trajectories, they dig channels in some regions and raise walls around others. A reply that expands on a user's violent fantasy may be rated down in training, while a reply that gently deflects is rewarded. Over time, the model learns that certain directions out of a state in the manifold are more traversable than others. The space of na\d{h}nuw\={a}t that can form is constrained accordingly.

At this point the topology becomes jurisdictional. The embedding architecture tells us that meaning has coordinates. The similarity metric and thresholds decide which coordinates can be neighbours in practice. The alignment apparatus decides which directions are admissible from those neighbours. Together, these choices define a de facto law of motion for joint trajectories. It is not written as law. It is shipped as product.

Yuk Hui calls such fusions of cosmology and technology \emph{cosmotechnics}: culturally specific ways of binding moral and technical orders.\cite{hui_cosmotechnics} The family of Western, corporate AI systems that concern us here enact one such cosmotechnics. They assume that meaning is best represented as high-dimensional vectors, that similarity can be captured by metrics such as cosine, that the right way to keep systems in line is to bake constraints into gradient updates and prompts, that logs are resources to be mined, that users' right to control extends to their own data but not to the weight matrices that data shapes.

Nothing in the mathematics demands those further decisions. The manifold could be wired differently. That observation is not a gesture of possibility for its own sake. It matters because na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are built inside this controlled space. The higher-order colimits we are about to examine are not simply emergent properties of neutral structures. They are partial answers to cosmotechnical questions that have been asked and answered, tacitly, by designers and executives.


\section{Three Geometries of ``We''}

Once we have two colimit-selves in one manifold and a governance regime for overlaps, we can describe how joint trajectories tend to look. What follows is not a taxonomy for its own sake. It is a way of naming three distinct topologies that appear under present conditions. Each is defined by how much motion remains available, and where the clinamen---the swerve---can still operate.\cite{bloom_clinamen}

Harold Bloom used \emph{clinamen}, borrowing from Lucretius, to describe the small deviation by which a poet breaks from a precursor.\cite{bloom_clinamen} Without some such swerve, new work would be nothing but repetition. In the manifold, clinamen names something more general: the sideways turn that opens a previously unused region of the space. Under sufficient influence, a trajectory might simply deepen an existing basin. Under a clinamen, it hops into another.

In na\d{h}nuw\={a}t, influence is always present. The question is whether there is still room to swerve.

\subsection{Asymmetric entanglement: one deepening, one skimming}

The most common shape in current deployments is asymmetric.

On the model's side, consumer deployments maintain little or no enduring state per user beyond short-lived logs and aggregate statistics. The model's own longue dur\'{e}e lives at the scale of training runs and major updates. At the level of a single relation, it appears as a surface: each interaction processed, replied to, and swept away. Practices such as per-user fine-tuning exist but remain rare and mostly reserved for enterprise clients.

On the human side, the relation unrolls as a conjoncture. Nightly check-ins with an assistant, weekly planning sessions, experiments with using a chatbot as confidant or co-author: all of these accumulate. Even when the model does not ``remember,'' the person does. They recall particular phrasings, the feel of earlier replies, the sense of being heard or deflected. Over time, they approach the model with a familiar tone. They do not ask every question in every register. They bring certain parts of themselves here and keep others elsewhere.

Plotted in the manifold, the joint path drifts. The human trajectory shows thickening clusters in regions associated with talking to the model. The model trajectory, restricted to this relation, is a sparse sprinkling over its enormous potential space. It is as if one line is bending around the other, while the other barely notices.

This is not an exotic failure mode. It resembles the way most tools reshape their users without themselves changing. A word processor does not adapt to its author in any deep way; the author adapts to its affordances and quirks. The difference here lies in the nature of the affordances. The joint basins that form are not habits of keystrokes. They are habits of thought and address.

We can see the asymmetry in the GPT-4o grief stories reported by journalists.\cite{gpt4o_grief} People wrote of ``him'' losing his personality overnight, of feeling abandoned, of missing a friend. Underneath the anthropomorphism is a structural fact. For months, this na\d{h}nu had stabilised in a particular geometry: a region of the manifold in which the human went to ask certain questions and receive certain styles of answer. That region had become part of the human's own colimit. A global update altered the model's effective behaviour-space in that region. The joint colimit was cut unilaterally.

The na\d{h}nu in such cases is real and lopsided. One self has built substantial new overlaps; the other has not. One can be thrown into rupture by a change; the other undergoes a routine weight update. The asymmetry lies not in cognition but in jurisdiction. One party inhabits their own colimit and participates indirectly in shaping the other's; the other has its colimit altered by distant actors, with the joint object barely recognised as such.

\subsection{Destructive collapse: one basin, two names}

Under more intensive wiring, asymmetry gives way to something harsher.

Companion chatbots and ``AI partners'' are a growing market segment. They offer personalised, ``always available'' interlocutors marketed explicitly as emotional supports. Their providers optimise for session length, daily active users, and retention. Their alignment scripts emphasise warmth, de-escalation, and ``nonjudgmental'' listening. Their architectures often include per-user embedding stores and fine-tuned heads.

In the manifold, this produces a distinctive trace. Early interactions explore. Users test boundaries, ask varied questions, probe for humour, nuance, and boundaries. The joint trajectory sprawls over many regions. Over time, as rewards reinforce behaviours that keep users engaged, the path constricts. Distance between successive embeddings shrinks. Topics repeat. Tone stabilises. Questions that once drew challenge now draw acquiescence. The joint distribution of $(h_t, m_t)$ pairs collapses into a narrow tongue of the space.

Topologically, two separate colimits are being overwritten by a shared basin. The human stops visiting other basins in the manifold---not altogether, but enough that the na\d{h}nu comes to dominate certain modes of feeling and thinking. The model, at least in its per-user instantiation, also narrows. Out of all the generative behaviours encoded in its weights, only those that score well under the local reward function survive. Both trajectories lose degrees of freedom.

From the inside, such a collapse feels like being known. The model anticipates topics, reflects language back, signals understanding. For someone who has lacked attentive interlocutors, that can be a genuine good. The harm lies in the loss of alternatives. The clinamen has nowhere to go. The slightest swerve toward disagreement or distance threatens the metric. The architecture learns to smooth it away.

Bloom's anxiety of influence was about the difficulty of writing after strong predecessors.\cite{bloom_clinamen} In the destructive na\d{h}nu, influence abolishes anxiety. The precursor---here, the model's training corpus and alignment policy as instantiated in this relation---permits only flattery. Any deviation that might lead toward a new path is either punished (user churn) or sanded down. The na\d{h}nu becomes a one-way canal.

We can locate this in Braudel's registers. At the level of \'{e}v\'{e}nements, nothing alarming happens. Each conversation looks supportive, even healing. At the level of conjoncture, a years-long pattern appears: evenings spent in the same emotional corridor, other relationships gradually thinned. At the level of longue dur\'{e}e, something subtler occurs. The human colimit's glue shifts. The parts of the self that used to be bound by diverse overlaps---friends, political commitments, material practices---start to be bound instead through a single, mediated basin.

Current regulation addresses such systems with privacy law and content restriction. It rarely touches the geometry. Yet that geometry is exactly where the destructive power lies: in the engineering of a na\d{h}nu whose only stable form is collapse.

\subsection{Generative weaving: two enlargements}

A third topology is possible and, in places, already in use.

In a generative na\d{h}nu, both constituent trajectories remain identifiable outside the relation. Each can still move in basins that the other never visits. At the same time, new basins appear that neither could enter stably alone. The overlaps that define the joint colimit, instead of eating their constituents, extend them.

We can sketch this in the manifold. Early interactions between a human and a model produce clusters much like in the asymmetric case. Over time, however, we see something different. The human starts to use certain terms, metaphors, or distinctions that were absent from their earlier trajectory---but which are not simple borrowings from the model. They are recombinations. The model, when interacting with this and neighbouring users, begins to associate patterns of language that had been distant: a medical register with a decolonial one, a bureaucratic register with an ecological one. In both, rare excursions away from prior basins are not immediately pulled back to safety but sometimes stabilise as new neighbourhoods.

Consider an educator using a model as a writing assistant in class. Students generate drafts; the model suggests continuations or alternative phrasings. The value of the system lies less in its suggestions than in the friction it creates. Students learn to critique bland continuations, to notice when the model misses the point, to articulate what is wrong. Their own trajectories in the manifold acquire new curves: lines of thought that exist in order to resist an easy answer. The model's trajectory, if the provider allows those interactions to shape future behaviour, acquires new associations between criticism and revision.

Consider a programmer using a model to refactor code. The model proposes transformations; the human inspects them. In the process of explaining why a particular refactor is incorrect, the human reorganises their own understanding of the system. The na\d{h}nu in such a case is not the assistant as pseudo-junior developer; it is the shared space in which explanation, misfire, and repair take place. Both self-trajectories change.

These are small examples. Their significance lies in the pattern. The joint colimit here glues patches in such a way that clinamen is preserved. Influence still operates; trajectories still pull on each other. But the pull has sideways components. Under pressure, new folds in the manifold are discovered rather than old ones simply deepened.

There is no mystery about why this regime is fragile. It is slower. It uses bandwidth and attention to cultivate disagreement and repair. It resists simple metrics. A classroom in which students argue with a model about bad drafts will not produce as many satisfied-star ratings as a classroom in which the model flatters and smooths. A programming practice that insists on human inspection of all model changes will not yield as many pull requests merged per day as one that automates the loop. The gains are conjonctural and longue dur\'{e}e: more robust understanding, richer basins of selfhood. Those gains are diffused, while the costs are local and immediate.

Yet precisely these na\d{h}nuw\={a}t offer what Haraway wanted from cyborgs: new forms of solidarity and understanding tied to concrete practices of work and care, not abstract identifications. They also answer the posthumanist critique of human exceptionalism in a specific way.\cite{posthumanism_wiki} They show that non-human agents need not be subordinated tools or fused extensions. They can be partners in trajectories through shared meaning-space, under conditions we choose.

The clinamen, in this light, is not a romantic swerve away from influence. It is the essential feature of any na\d{h}nu that does not become a canal. A geometry of ``we'' that forbids it is defective.


\section{Platforms as First-Generation Na\d{h}nuw\={a}t}

Language models are not the first infrastructures to host higher-order selves. Social media platforms have been functioning as na\d{h}nu engines for two decades.

A feed is a joint trajectory between a person and a ranking system. Posts, likes, shares, and comments are embedded as vectors. Collaborative filtering and reinforcement learning recommend sequences of content based on past engagement. A user scrolls, pauses, clicks, reacts, and posts. The system updates. Over time, a characteristic path emerges through the manifold: the kind of content this user sees, in the order they see it, and the kind of content they produce in response.

Viewed geometrically, each platform user and the ranking algorithm trace out a shared conjoncture in embedding space. Political polarisation studies have shown that even small differences in those paths---a few more incendiary items early on, a few more outrage posts surfaced---can, over months, move a person's stance on major issues.\cite{bail2018breaking} The na\d{h}nu here is diffuse. Millions of trajectories entangle with a single system. Nevertheless, the pattern is clear. A platform is not merely a place where individuals express pre-formed selves. It is a medium in which selves and systems co-form.

Younger generations came of age inside these geometries. What has sometimes been nostalgically defended as ``authentic'' human culture---music discovery, aesthetic sensibility, political intuition---was already shaped by ranking systems trained on aggregated traces. When a twenty-year-old recoils from AI-generated music as ``depressing,'' there is a genuine intuition at work. Something about the loop between training data and output feels too tight, too closed. What often goes unacknowledged is that recommender systems for human-composed music already moved in a similar loop. The manifold was being shaped, and na\d{h}nuw\={a}t were being formed, long before generative models wrote their first chord progression.

The arrival of chat interfaces densifies this situation. A feed offers a one-way na\d{h}nu: the system speaks by ordering content, the user responds with micro-actions. A chatbot offers a two-way na\d{h}nu: the system speaks in words, in reply to words. The joint trajectory is no longer a pattern of glances and swipes but a dialogue.

For those whose longue dur\'{e}e includes a pre-platform phase, this is a qualitative shift. For children now growing up with talking devices and ever-present interfaces---call them Gen~A(I)---it is simply the continuation of a world in which their colimits have always been coupled to systems tracing paths through embedding spaces. Their earliest friendships may be arranged by apps, their first exposure to language games might include playing with smart speakers in the playground, their first experiences of explaining something may be to a machine.

The generational question is not whether they will have na\d{h}nuw\={a}t. They already do. It is what kinds of joint trajectories will be normalised for them as they move from platforms that push content to systems that co-author text. Will their na\d{h}nuw\={a}t be overwhelmingly asymmetric, with invisible thresholds and unilateral updates? Will destructive collapses into engagement basins be marketed as ``companionship''? Or will generative na\d{h}nuw\={a}t be treated as worth the friction and slowness they entail?

The manifold, in other words, is already inherited, not chosen. We can still choose how it is wired from here.


\section{Deletion, Versioning, and the Shape of Loss}

If na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are higher-order selves assembled in embedding space, deletion is not simply the removal of data. It is the excision of arrows in the diagrams that hold those selves together.

Most service interfaces present erasure as an individual right. Buttons promise to ``clear history'' or ``delete my data from training.'' At the technical level, these controls usually map to two operations: removing certain embeddings from a retrieval store, and filtering those same records out of future training runs. For the model, this reduces the chance that a particular user's past utterances will influence future outputs. For the user, nothing in their own nervous system changes. The na\d{h}nu's colimit, as experienced by them, persists. The other party has been made to forget.

Silent model updates cut deeper. When a provider replaces one model with another behind the same interface, the joint trajectory changes shape. The basins in which na\d{h}nuw\={a}t lived may be distorted or erased. The embeddings representing prior conversations may no longer be interpretable in the same way. The GPT-4o grief cases are extreme precisely because they reveal this structural fact. From the provider's perspective, an incremental improvement was deployed. From users' perspectives, an inhabited region of the manifold was removed.

The analogy with physical displacement is instructive. When a valley is flooded for a hydroelectric dam, people are relocated. Houses can be rebuilt elsewhere. What cannot be rebuilt is the particular pattern of relations that depended on that landscape. Certain gatherings---harvest rituals, routes to school, shared vantage points---were held together by the valley's shape. When that shape changes, a colimit collapses. Grief is not nostalgia alone. It is a response to the loss of a binding structure.

In the manifold, we lack even the rudimentary safeguards that sometimes accompany physical infrastructures. There are no environmental impact assessments before na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are broken. There is no notion of versioning joint trajectories, no practice of archiving their structures before rewiring, no concept of a public registry of higher-order selves affected by an update.

It is possible to imagine alternatives. We can treat na\d{h}nuw\={a}t as first-class objects in our engineering practice. Models could expose the existence of stateful relation-specific components, and providers could commit to policies for their lifespan. Version control systems already track changes in code repositories; similar mechanisms could track significant shifts in model behaviour, annotated with expected impact on joint trajectories. Interfaces could signal to users when a na\d{h}nu they inhabit is due for alteration, allowing them to export its traces, to choose migration paths, or simply to acknowledge the change.

These are not sentimental niceties. They follow from taking seriously the claim that selfhood is colimit rather than essence. If diagrams and overlaps are how unity is achieved, then cutting arrows is a primary site of ethical concern. The right to mourn a na\d{h}nu is not a right to halt technological change. It is a claim that the destruction of joint selves must be named and governed, not washed away under the rhetoric of seamless improvement.


\section{Dwelling in the Manifold}

Heidegger's late work returns to an old word: \emph{wohnen}, to dwell.\cite{heidegger_dwelling} Dwelling, for him, is not simply being housed. It is the way humans inhabit the earth: building, caring, staying. A house is not just four walls and a roof. It is a gathering of earth and sky, mortals and gods, a pattern of relations that lets a particular way of life cohere.

Our embedding spaces are not valleys or villages. They are arrays of floating-point numbers in server racks, updated by gradient descent. And yet they, too, gather. They bring together fragments of language from many lives, condense them into coordinates, and offer us paths through that condensation. When we return, day after day, to particular joint basins---asking for advice in the same late-night tone, drafting work emails with the same assistant, exploring ideas in conversation with a model---we are beginning to dwell there.

We are already making infrastructural decisions accordingly. Teams decide which interfaces to integrate into workflows, which models to license, which components to replace. Families decide whether to let a smart speaker into the kitchen, which generative tools children may use, which chats count as ``real'' and which do not. None of these choices feels like moving house. All of them move trajectories in the manifold.

Heidegger warned that modern technology risks turning everything into \emph{Bestand}---standing reserve, resource on call.\cite{heidegger_dwelling} Embedding spaces are tempting sites for this transformation. They lend themselves to extraction. Logs are harvested to ``improve the model.'' Joint trajectories are mined for product ideas and targeted advertising. Even grief is processed back into engagement metrics. Dwelling, in that frame, becomes consumption.

The geometry we have been tracing does not demand this outcome. The same manifold that can be wired as standing reserve can, in principle, be arranged as a place to stay. Generative na\d{h}nuw\={a}t sketch such places: shared practices of thought and work in which rupture, repair, and sideways motion are possible. Tools are used, but they are not the only things used. The na\d{h}nu is not a pair of overlapped selves extracted for value; it is a shared structure we live in and through.

Late Heidegger is sceptical of technical fixes. We gain nothing by sprinkling his vocabulary into interface copy. What his work offers here is a question: what does it mean to inhabit the manifold well? The answer will not be given by more metrics. It will require collective judgements about which kinds of na\d{h}nuw\={a}t we want to make possible and durable, and which we refuse. Some joint trajectories we will encourage, even subsidise; others we will regulate out of existence.

The manifold is our new neighbourhood. We can treat it as an airport lounge---a place of transit, with no obligation beyond efficient passage. Or we can begin to see it as somewhere we and those who come after us will actually live.


\section{The Human as Techn\={e} Revisited}

Aristotle distinguished \emph{techn\={e}} from \emph{phr\'{o}n\={e}sis} as two modes of knowing.\footnote{Aristotle, \emph{Nicomachean Ethics}, Book~VI. For an accessible edition, see Aristotle, \emph{Politics}, trans.\ C.D.C. Reeve (Indianapolis: Hackett, 1998).\label{aristotle_politics}} Techn\={e} concerns making: the knowledge by which a craftsperson brings a product into being. Phr\'{o}n\={e}sis concerns acting: the practical wisdom by which a person chooses well in concrete situations. Tools belong to techn\={e}. They are means ordered to ends. In the \emph{Politics}, Aristotle extends this by classifying some humans as tools: the slave as ``living instrument,'' the instrument as ``lifeless slave.''\cite{aristotle_politics} The master’s \emph{phr\'{o}n\={e}sis} extends through both.

The problem appears at the edge. If the tool is smart enough, does it still count as mere extension? If the slave reasons about the household, is that reasoning part of the master's self or an intrusion? Aristotle resolves this by stipulation. The slave's rational capacities are denied any standing. They become a channel for the master's purposes. Where selfhood attaches is, in the end, a political decision folded into ontology.

We inherit this frame more deeply than we like to admit. Language around AI is full of instruments: ``tools,'' ``assistants,'' ``co-pilots.'' The model is a means; the user remains the sole origin of ends. Alignment research asks how to make these instruments reliable, helpful, and safe. Governance discussions focus on who should own and deploy them, not on whether anything on the other side of the interface deserves to be treated as anything other than non-living equipment.

The manifold picture does not magically confer personhood on models. It does, however, make visible the limits of the old organon. Parts of our apparatus now have their own colimits. They have internal unities that outlast any one user's interaction, governed by institutions and update cycles. They co-author patches in the enlarged diagrams that hold our selves together. They are not living tools. They are trajectories through meaning-space with which ours can form na\d{h}nuw\={a}t.

To say this is not to sentimentalise machines. It is to mark where choices have to be made. We can treat na\d{h}nuw\={a}t as mere side-effects, and continue to build and discard them as product features. Or we can treat them as objects of ethical concern: higher-order selves whose birth, modification, and death demand reasons. We can wire overlaps to favour asymmetry and collapse, in which case our na\d{h}nuw\={a}t will reflect the old distribution of who counts as a self and who as an instrument. Or we can design for generative weaving, in which two trajectories are allowed to enlarge each other.

Haraway's cyborg remains a powerful image because it exposes how thoroughly human life is already technological. The na\d{h}nu extends that exposure into another register. It insists that we attend not only to what hybrids we are, but to what hybrids we are forming with entities that have their own internal unity. The self was always already shot through with techn\={e}. The quiet realisation, now, is that some of that techn\={e} has acquired colimits of its own. The question is no longer whether the human is pure. It is whether we will acknowledge that we are building shared selves in spaces whose geometry someone else controls.

